    printf "playground connector smt test\n\n"
    printf "  🧪 Apply the transform chain (SMT) of a connector to a record, step by step\n  \n  Shows what each transform changes (topic, key, value, headers, schema field by\n  field), the result of each predicate, a record dropped by a transform, and the\n  transform that throws with its config and its error. No topic or connector is\n  created: the chain runs in a small harness in the connect container, with the\n  classpath and the plugin.path of the worker, so the transforms, predicates and\n  converters are the same classes and versions as in the worker.\n  \n  The transforms come from a running connector (--connector) or from a connector\n  config on stdin (or --config @file). The record is a json (--record):\n  schemaless, or {\"schema\": ..., \"payload\": ...} like the JsonConverter for a\n  record with a schema. Or a real record of a topic (--from-topic, --offset),\n  converted with the converters of the connector (avro, protobuf, json schema\n  with schema registry).\n  \n  --cp-version runs the same test with the built-in transforms of other CP\n  versions (throwaway cp-server-connect containers), --smt-plugin installs a\n  Confluent Hub plugin for the test only (the worker is not changed), --smt-jar\n  adds a jar (custom SMT development).\n  \n  An html report of the steps (the value after each step with the added, changed\n  and removed fields highlighted, the schema diff, the predicates, the error\n  with its stack trace, and with --cp-version a comparison of the versions) is\n  written to /tmp/playground-smt-test, and opened in the browser in an\n  interactive terminal.\n  \n  🎓 Tip: the connector config can be a whole connector config: only\n  transforms.*, predicates.* and the converters are used.\n\n"
  else
    printf "playground connector smt test - 🧪 Apply the transform chain (SMT) of a connector to a record, step by step\n\n"
